      "date_text": "Tue 3 Nov 2026, 19:00",
      "description": "\n  <p><span>Senegalese practice Worofila presents this year's Rick Mather lecture, celebrating the work of architects who are making important contributions to thinking about sustainability. </span></p>\n\n<p><span>Led by <strong>Nzinga Mboup</strong> and <strong>Nicolas Rondet</strong>, Worofila is an architecture practice based in Dakar. Its work focuses on designing buildings that work with the local climate and make use of locally sourced, low-impact materials, traditional knowledge, and simple, community-led ways of building and maintaining them.</span></p><p><span>The lecture coincides with the publication of </span><em><span>Ecosystems: A Contemporary African Architecture</span></em><span> (MACK), which brings together Worofila’s work to date, from housing and public buildings to infrastructure and spaces for learning and experimentation.</span></p>",
      "ends": null,
