  {
    "event": null,
    "id": "2026/robert-ryman-a-new-way-of-seeing-courtauld-symposium",
    "payload": {
      "art_forms": [
        "art-design"
      ],
      "date_text": "Fri 20 Nov 2026, 15:00",
      "description": "\n  <p><span>This half-day symposium revisits the American painter Robert Ryman’s work, connections and legacy from a range of perspectives, marking the major retrospective of his work in our Art Gallery.</span></p>\n\n<p><span>For almost 60 years, Ryman devoted himself to making paintings without pictorial or symbolic content, instead focusing his attention on the physical properties of materials and their effects. In works that range from intimate to monumental, and flat to three-dimensional, Ryman explored the basic assumptions of painting – that it involves paint, on a surface, hung on a wall. His body of work is marked equally by surprise and subtlety, sameness and difference, and by shifting uses of paint, gesture, support, size, line, fastenings to the wall and space.</span></p>",
      "ends": null,
      "image_url": "https://www.barbican.org.uk/sites/default/files/styles/event_listing_small_jpg/public/images/2026-06/84%20Maeght%20Paris%20Nguyen%20A.jpg?h=13b90b22&itok=jFAb97P9",
      "listing_title": "Robert Ryman: A New Way of Seeing – Courtauld Symposium",
      "price_text": "Free",
      "room": "The Courtauld at Vernon Square, Lecture Theatre 2",
      "series": [
        "robert-ryman-talks-and-events"
      ],
      "starts": "2026-11-20T15:00:00Z",
      "subtitle": "Courtauld Symposium",
      "title": "Robert Ryman: A New Way of Seeing",
      "url": "https://www.barbican.org.uk/whats-on/2026/event/robert-ryman-a-new-way-of-seeing-courtauld-symposium"
    }
  },
  {
    "event": null,
    "id": "2026/robert-ryman-exhibition-tour-with-curatorial-assistant",
    "payload": {
      "art_forms": [
        "art-design"
      ],
      "date_text": "Fri 6 Nov 2026, 18:30",
      "description": "\n  <p><span>Join Curatorial Assistant Amber Li for a guided tour of Robert Ryman: The Real Thing, focusing on some of her personal highlights.</span></p>\n\n<p><span>The Real Thing is the first major UK exhibition of Robert Ryman's paintings in over 30 years. Emerging as an artist in New York in the mid-1950s, Ryman approached every painting as a test to ‘see what would happen’, discovering that ‘there is never a question of what to paint, but only how to paint.’ For almost six decades, Ryman mainly used white paint and a square format to test materials and their effects, producing a remarkable variety of paintings that affirm materials as they are and ask us to pay attention to subtlety and nuance.</span></p><p><span>Presenting over 60 works from the mid-1950s to the early 2010s, this exhibition celebrates an artist who believed the poetry of painting was in feeling, and that painting was best understood though real, in-person experience:&nbsp;‘the real thing…was the only way to do it.’</span></p>",
      "ends": null,
      "image_url": "https://www.barbican.org.uk/sites/default/files/styles/event_listing_small_jpg/public/images/2026-01/Untitled%20design%20%285%29.png?h=7adbbeb1&itok=7i62-J5Y",
      "listing_title": null,
      "price_text": "£20.50 (£19 + £1.50 transaction fee)",
      "room": "Art Gallery",
      "series": [
        "robert-ryman-talks-and-events"
      ],
      "starts": "2026-11-06T18:30:00Z",
      "subtitle": "Robert Ryman: The Real Thing",
      "title": "Exhibition Tour with Curatorial Assistant Amber Li",
      "url": "https://www.barbican.org.uk/whats-on/2026/event/robert-ryman-exhibition-tour-with-curatorial-assistant"
    }
  },
  {
    "event": {
      "address": "Silk Street, London EC2Y 8DS",
      "all_day": false,
      "category": "exhibition",
      "dedupe_key": "robert-ryman-real-thing|2026-10-08|barbican",
      "description": "‘You have to see the real thing… seeing a real painting is the only way to do it.’ Robert Ryman believed that the best way to experience a painting is in person. His paintings, which mostly use white paint and a square format, reward close looking. He wanted you to notice everything: the texture of the paint, the brushstrokes, the material being painted on, how it's displayed on the wall. The magic is in the feelings that arise as you attune yourself to the paintings’ similarities and differences, how they relate to each other and interact with the surrounding light and space. Presenting over 60 works made from the mid-1950s to the early 2010s, this major new retrospective of the extraordinary American artist invites you to take a closer look. You may notice aspects of painting you haven’t seen before – and perhaps you’ll leave wondering what else in the world might become clearer if you simply slow down and tune in.",
      "ends_at": "2027-02-28T17:00:00Z",
      "image_url": "https://www.barbican.org.uk/sites/default/files/styles/event_listing_small_jpg/public/images/2026-01/Ryman%20web%20image2.png?h=7adbbeb1&itok=lTYQr3bz",
      "lat": 51.5201,
      "lng": -0.0955,
      "price": {
